---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="mhs — Active fractal"
  description="Quasi-static Coulomb loading of a synthetic 3-D fault network: 512 faults, 10230 mollified elements, and the subset that actually slips as the regional load rises."
  current="fractal"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>Active fractal<span class="sub">which faults actually slip</span></h1>
    <p>
      A synthetic fault network with no slip prescribed anywhere. Each element
      carries a frictional strength, and slip is permitted only where the
      resolved stress reaches it. The regional load rises; faults activate,
      their stress changes load every other fault, and the network finds a new
      equilibrium.
    </p>
    <p>
      512 faults on a multiplicative cascade in a 40&nbsp;km cube,
      10,230&nbsp;elements, <i>&epsilon;</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;10&nbsp;m. Strike-slip
      regime, &mu;<sub>s</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.6 with a 10&nbsp;% strength drop,
      effective normal stress 150&nbsp;MPa.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <b>This is the experiment the on-fault interaction matrix exists for.</b>
      A stress-driven network needs <i>K<sub>ii</sub></i> &mdash; how much an
      element's own slip relieves its own shear &mdash; and for a classical
      dislocation element that number is undefined on the fault. Mollification
      makes it finite and, measured here, negative on every one of the 10,230
      elements.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      The slider is the <b>load parameter</b>, not time. Every frame is an
      equilibrium: all elements locked, every shear stress below its static
      strength. Nothing in this animation propagates.
    </p>
    <div class="links">
      <div><a href={`${base}concepts/`}>How the kernel works</a></div>
      <div><a href="https://github.com/brendanjmeade/mhs/tree/main/studies/active_fractal">The study code</a></div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="mode"><span>colour</span></label>
      <span data-ctl="play"></span>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="frame"><span>load</span></div>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="A synthetic three-dimensional fault network of 512 faults, coloured by which elements slip as the regional load increases"></div>
    <div data-bar class="bar"></div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What is and is not being claimed</h2>

  <p>
    The machinery works and the numbers above are measured: 78,913 events over
    the load range shown, 73.7&nbsp;% of elements eventually slipping, a
    cumulative moment of 6.4&times;10<sup>17</sup>&nbsp;N&nbsp;m, and the
    largest single load increment moving 2,329 elements at once. Every event
    reached equilibrium; none ran away.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>The headline question is not answered on this page.</b> Asking whether
    slip organises onto a lower-dimensional subset than the geometry offers
    requires comparing the active set against a subset of the <i>same size</i>
    drawn without regard to mechanics &mdash; because box-counting dimension
    falls when a set simply has fewer members. Measured on a sheet that is
    exactly two-dimensional, a 5&nbsp;% random subset reads 0.36 lower and a
    1&nbsp;% subset loses a full dimension. So &ldquo;the active set is
    lower-dimensional&rdquo; is nearly free, for a reason with no mechanics in
    it, and the null models are the entire result rather than a formality.
    Those are built and calibrated; the comparison is not yet run.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two honest limits on what the dimension can ever say here. The estimator is
    biased low by about 0.11 on an absolute dimension and that bias is
    <i>invisible to R<sup>2</sup></i> &mdash; every calibration fit has
    R<sup>2</sup>&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0.999, including one wrong by 0.14 &mdash; so
    only differences in a matched window are quoted, which are good to about
    0.05. And the window itself is bounded below by the <i>largest fault</i>
    rather than the element size, because until a box is bigger than a fault
    that fault reads locally two-dimensional. For this network that leaves 0.73
    decades, enough to rank against a null and not enough to quote three digits.
  </p>

  <h2>Why there is a strength drop</h2>

  <p>
    With a single friction coefficient this experiment has no mechanism. The
    quasi-static solution is then <b>continuous</b> in the load: an element
    reaching its failure criterion begins to slip at zero rate, no event has a
    finite size, and there is nothing to count. The first version of the solver
    demonstrated exactly that, advancing the load to the first failure and then
    relaxing zero slip indefinitely.
  </p>

  <p>
    So an element has a static strength it must reach to fail and a lower
    dynamic strength it relaxes down to, and the difference between them is
    what loads its neighbours and can trigger them in turn. The ratio between
    the two is the single most important physical parameter on this page; at
    1.0 there are no cascades by construction, and the code refuses that value
    rather than returning a null result that would look like a finding.
  </p>

  <h2>What the solver choice costs</h2>

  <p>
    A cascade has no unique end state &mdash; a triggered element lands anywhere
    between its dynamic and static strength depending on the order elements are
    visited &mdash; so there is no exact answer to validate against. What can be
    done is to vary the update rule and measure the spread. Between
    Gauss-Seidel and Jacobi, event counts move by 6.9&nbsp;% and mean event
    size by 4.3&nbsp;%; between element orderings, under 1&nbsp;%. But total
    slip is invariant to 0.1&nbsp;%, and the <b>active set came out bit
    identical</b> under every rule tried &mdash; symmetric difference zero. So
    the quantity the dimension is measured on carries no solver dependence,
    while an avalanche exponent carries that 7&nbsp;% as part of its
    uncertainty.
  </p>

  <h2>One simplification worth naming</h2>

  <p>
    Effective normal stress is <b>uniform with depth</b> here, and that is a
    deliberate choice rather than an oversight. The real gradient is about
    0.0167&nbsp;GPa/km once hydrostatic pore pressure is removed, which across
    a 40&nbsp;km box is a factor of twenty in strength: the shallow elements
    would fail first, the active set would be a horizontal slab, and its
    measured dimension would be reporting the depth gradient rather than any
    mechanical localisation. Removing it leaves orientation and elastic
    interaction as the only things that can select which faults slip, which is
    the question. The depth-dependent case runs, and is important future work
    &mdash; it needs its own null, the same network with the gradient and the
    elastic interaction switched off, or depth and localisation stay confounded.
  </p>

  <p class="cap">
    Slip is stored quantised: log<sub>10</sub> into one byte per element per
    frame, 254 levels, zero reserved for &ldquo;has never slipped&rdquo; and
    drawn grey rather than at the bottom of the ramp, because a locked element
    is categorically different from one that slipped a little. The whole
    120&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;10,230 array is encoded in one pass, so the colour
    scale is global across frames &mdash; a per-frame scale would normalise away
    the growth being animated. The quantisation error is bounded by arithmetic
    rather than asserted, and the build measures what it wrote against that
    bound: 0.898&nbsp;% against a bound of 0.898&nbsp;%, a ratio of 1.000.
  </p>

  <style is:global>
    #viewer { margin-bottom: 18px; }
    #viewer .canvas { width: 100%; min-height: 420px; cursor: grab; background: #fff; }
    #viewer .canvas:active { cursor: grabbing; }
    #viewer .canvas canvas { display: block; }
    #viewer .panel {
      display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px 18px; align-items: center;
      margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 13px;
    }
    #viewer .panel label { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; }
    #viewer .panel label > span:first-child { color: var(--fg-dim); }
    #viewer .panel select { font: inherit; font-size: 13px; padding: 2px 4px; }
    #viewer .panel .rng { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; flex: 1; min-width: 220px; }
    #viewer .panel .rng > span:first-child { color: var(--fg-dim); }
    #viewer .panel input[type="range"] { flex: 1; min-width: 120px; }
    #viewer .panel .val { color: var(--fg-dim); min-width: 92px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
    #viewer .bar { margin-top: 8px; }
    #viewer .bar .strip { height: 10px; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    #viewer .bar .ends {
      display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px;
      color: var(--fg-dim); margin-top: 3px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    }
    #viewer .bar .ends .mid { color: var(--fg-faint); }
    #viewer .status { font-size: 13px; color: var(--fg-dim); margin-top: 8px; line-height: 1.5; }
    .cap { font-size: 13px; color: var(--fg-dim); }
    .cap b { color: var(--fg); font-weight: 600; }
  </style>
  <script>
    import { startFractal } from "../viz/fractal";
    startFractal(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
